===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=be|:inh|enm:aren|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|aren}}, from {{inh|en|ang|earun|q=Anglian}}, {{m|ang|earon|t=are|pos=plural}}. Possibly reinforced by the {{cog|non|-}} plural forms in {{m|non||er-}}, this displaced the alternative forms {{cog|ang|sind}} and {{m|ang|bēoþ}}. In the second person singular it displaced archaic {{m|en|art|id=you are}}. Further etymology {{unc|en|controversial}}:
* The English forms, as well as the {{cog|gmq-osw|-}} forms in {{m|gmq-osw||ær-}}, could reflect {{inh|en|gem-pro|-}} {{glossary|preterite-present}} {{m|gem-pro|*ōr}} ~ {{m|gem-pro|*arum}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₃er-|alt=*h₃e-h₃ór-h₂e|t=I have risen|pos=perfect}}.<ref>{{R:gem:PIEPG|page=72}}. Note: in the 2nd edition (2017) this example has been omitted.</ref><ref>{{R:ine:LIV|page=300|head=''*h₃er-''}}</ref>
* Since they are not the expected outcomes of the {{inh|en|gem-pro|-}} forms of {{m|gem-pro|*wesaną|t=to be}} in {{m|gem-pro|*iz-}}, they would have to be irregular alterations. For Seebold this explanation is still preferable as similar variants in other verbs are not uncommon.<ref>{{R:gem:Seebold|80f.|head=AR (?) ‘ist’}}</ref>

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|en|aren|arn||obsolete|_|or|_|dialectal}}
* {{alt|en|ar||obsolete}}

